  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Other Management Consulting Services (NAICS 541618) shows reduced federal contract activity in FY2026 to date. Total obligations are $147M across 560 awards, with an average award size of $262K. Obligations fell 82.6% year over year. Historic obligations peaked in FY24 at $1.3B and then dropped to $843M in FY25 before reaching the current FY26 to date total of $147M. The Department of Defense and the Department of Health and Human Services are the largest buying agencies so far, with obligations of $52.7M and $46.8M respectively. ISLAND PEER REVIEW ORGANIZATION INC, PALANTIR USG INC, and BATTELLE MEMORIAL INSTITUTE lead vendors by obligations in FY2026 to date, holding $52.4M, $27.1M, and $14.4M respectively. Buyers and vendors beyond the top few show much smaller FY2026 to date obligations, with the General Services Administration at $3.5M and the tenth-ranked vendor at $3.8M. This snapshot covers fiscal-year-to-date obligations and reflects partial-year totals.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What explains the year-over-year decline in obligations?",
        "body": "The timing of contract awards and demand shifts explain the lower FY2026 to date total. Fiscal history shows obligations of $1.3B in FY24 and $843M in FY25 before the current total of $147M in FY26 to date. Concentration of large awards in prior years makes the current partial-year comparison appear sharply lower. Agency buying patterns, where the Department of Defense and Department of Health and Human Services account for the largest shares so far, also affect year-over-year totals."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors matter most for bidders now?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense and the Department of Health and Human Services matter most, with obligations of $52.7M and $46.8M respectively in FY2026 to date. ISLAND PEER REVIEW ORGANIZATION INC and PALANTIR USG INC matter most among vendors, with obligations of $52.4M and $27.1M respectively. Other agencies and vendors show much smaller FY2026 to date obligations, so competition and opportunity differ by buyer and incumbent presence. Monitoring award activity by those top buyers and vendors will show where current dollars are concentrated."
      }
    ]
  },
